WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.501
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:04.267
We are here with Catherine
Zeta-Jones.

00:00:04.267 --> 00:00:05.501
And this is very exciting.

00:00:05.501 --> 00:00:07.601
You mentioned that you're
playing Morticia Addams

00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:10.267
in the fantastic new series
"Wednesday."

00:00:10.267 --> 00:00:12.200
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:14.767
-With my co-star Fred.
-Yeah, Fred.

00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:17.300
Fred is in it, as well,
as Uncle Fester.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:21.133
And I would not --
Fred, I should stress,

00:00:21.133 --> 00:00:23.968
I would not have thought
you'd be a perfect Uncle Fester

00:00:23.968 --> 00:00:25.133
until I saw the photo.

00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:27.033
I wasn't like --
In all the years I knew you,

00:00:27.033 --> 00:00:28.968
I wasn't like, "Oh,
you should be a Fester one day,

00:00:28.968 --> 00:00:30.567
but, my God, nailed it."

00:00:30.567 --> 00:00:31.834
-Nailed it.
-Thanks. Thank you.

00:00:31.834 --> 00:00:35.467
-Yeah. You guys did not have
any scenes together.

00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:38.467
-No, we didn't,
but it was a real treat.

00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:41.234
You know, I mean, a bit like
the show I'm working --

00:00:41.234 --> 00:00:45.133
"National Treasure,"
it's like an iconic show that --

00:00:45.133 --> 00:00:48.634
and characters like Fester
and Morticia that are really --

00:00:48.634 --> 00:00:50.367
It's more than
pop culture, really.

00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:53.133
They're, like, indelible.
They're in our subconscious.

00:00:53.133 --> 00:00:56.434
My niece knows
"The Addams Family"

00:00:56.434 --> 00:00:57.701
and Wednesday in particular.

00:00:57.701 --> 00:00:59.434
And my grandmother,
God bless her soul,

00:00:59.434 --> 00:01:00.667
sits on my shoulder.

00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:02.634
She loved "The Addams Family."

00:01:02.634 --> 00:01:04.634
So, it's -- And then,
you know, you throw in

00:01:04.634 --> 00:01:06.868
the brilliant mind of Tim Burton

00:01:06.868 --> 00:01:10.300
and the fantastic casting of
Jenna Ortega,

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:12.834
and, you know, you --
I'm just thrilled

00:01:12.834 --> 00:01:14.367
that, you know,
it's done so well

00:01:14.367 --> 00:01:15.901
and people are really
enjoying the work.

00:01:15.901 --> 00:01:18.534
-Yeah, Jenna Ortega is amazing,
and for those who don't know,

00:01:18.534 --> 00:01:20.300
it sort of is about Wednesday
going off to a boarding school.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:21.734
-Yeah.

00:01:21.734 --> 00:01:23.734
-So you're playing a mom who's
dropping the kids off at school.

00:01:23.734 --> 00:01:25.334
You know, we were talking about
your kids a little bit.

00:01:25.334 --> 00:01:26.901
Did it bring you back to
the first time

00:01:26.901 --> 00:01:30.400
you sort of sent them away
to make their own lives?

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.000
-Yes. I mean, 'cause with
all the kind of iconic curves

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.901
and nuances that Morticia has.

00:01:35.901 --> 00:01:37.367
She is the matriarch, you know?

00:01:37.367 --> 00:01:39.667
And so
it was something wonderful

00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:42.167
about playing that matriarch.

00:01:42.167 --> 00:01:44.167
I was just a bawling wreck.

00:01:44.167 --> 00:01:47.267
I was embarrassing when my kids
went off to college, you know?

00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:49.100
You try and hold it together

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.534
and you tell them,
"Now, don't get upset."

00:01:51.534 --> 00:01:53.267
And then, the next minute,
you're like a --

00:01:53.267 --> 00:01:54.534
you know, a fool.

00:01:54.534 --> 00:01:58.701
And, you know, you just --
Anyway, but it's amazing,

00:01:58.701 --> 00:02:01.801
yeah, those milestones
in your life.

00:02:01.801 --> 00:02:04.801
And, you know, with Jenna, this
mother-daughter relationship

00:02:04.801 --> 00:02:06.968
is really crucial
to the whole show, you know?

00:02:06.968 --> 00:02:08.767
-Well, people are
really responding to it.

00:02:08.767 --> 00:02:10.100
And it's so cool,
it's so exciting

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:11.701
anytime Tim Burton
does something.

00:02:11.701 --> 00:02:13.100
-I know.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:14.501
-You were saying, you know,
you worked with Fred.

00:02:14.501 --> 00:02:16.467
You guys were all in Romania
working on this.

00:02:16.467 --> 00:02:18.100
But during the commercial break,
you were saying

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:19.400
you did not know
Fred played drums.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.400
-No.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:21.501
-And then you shared with us

00:02:21.501 --> 00:02:23.834
that you were
a British tap champion.

00:02:23.834 --> 00:02:26.734
-Yeah. Yeah. Yeah.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:26.734 --> 00:02:28.868
And tapping -- The reason why
I was saying to Fred,

00:02:28.868 --> 00:02:30.334
'cause I didn't know
he played drums.

00:02:30.334 --> 00:02:31.834
You know, he's got too much
talent in one body.

00:02:31.834 --> 00:02:33.200
-Yeah.
-That's okay.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:38.868
-But I dabble with drums,
I play piano, and I love music.

00:02:38.868 --> 00:02:43.100
But when I tap-dance, it's
really like I'm playing drums.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:45.701
And I always thought that if
I was in a band,

00:02:45.701 --> 00:02:47.634
I would -- You know,
I wouldn't play drums.

00:02:47.634 --> 00:02:50.100
I would just tap the rhythm.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:53.434
But I'm waiting for a show where
I can tap-dance -- or a film.

00:02:53.434 --> 00:02:55.300
-I mean,
we're throwing it out there.

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.200
-With "Chicago,"
I didn't get to tap.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.100
I did think about if
I could slip it in somewhere.

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.334
But -- Yeah.

00:03:00.334 --> 00:03:02.701
-What was the competition like
when you won

00:03:02.701 --> 00:03:04.701
your British tap championship?

00:03:04.701 --> 00:03:06.200
-Well, it was all
the dancing schools

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:07.234
from around the country,

00:03:07.234 --> 00:03:08.634
and, you know,
you all kind of compete.

00:03:08.634 --> 00:03:09.834
-Did you get nervous?

00:03:09.834 --> 00:03:12.534
Do you get nervous
in the competition setting

00:03:12.534 --> 00:03:14.234
when you were
a young tap dancer?

00:03:14.234 --> 00:03:16.200
-I didn't get nervous
for anything when I was young,

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.501
but as soon as you reach kind of
15, 16, all of a sudden,

00:03:19.501 --> 00:03:21.267
the older you get,
the more nervous I get.

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:23.300
I still get nervous
the first day of shooting.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:25.033
But when I was a kid,
I didn't get nervous.

00:03:25.033 --> 00:03:27.601
-Did your peer group
think tap was cool?

00:03:27.601 --> 00:03:29.367
-Yeah.
-Oh, that's pretty awesome.

00:03:29.367 --> 00:03:31.033
-Oh, yeah, they thought
it was very cool.

00:03:31.033 --> 00:03:34.234
-Well, you grew up in Wales, and
I don't know if you ever heard,

00:03:34.234 --> 00:03:36.400
Dylan Thomas once said
it was a musical nation.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.234
-You just stole my line.
-I know that.

00:03:40.234 --> 00:03:42.968
-You are so smart.
-That's a thing I know.

00:03:42.968 --> 00:03:45.501
-You are so -- He is so smart.

00:03:45.501 --> 00:03:47.400
You know, it's like,
nine years on the show,

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:48.667
I thought the brain cells
would have gone.

00:03:48.667 --> 00:03:50.033
Nope.

00:03:50.033 --> 00:03:53.767
-No, they're growing every year.
I just absorb information.

00:03:53.767 --> 00:03:56.968
I heard there's one role
that got away from you,

00:03:56.968 --> 00:03:58.300
which was Bond.

00:03:58.300 --> 00:04:00.067
-I would have loved to
have played Bond.

00:04:00.067 --> 00:04:01.467
-See? That's so exciting.

00:04:01.467 --> 00:04:03.100
-Because, you know,
in Britain --

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.868
I guess it's in America, too.

00:04:04.868 --> 00:04:06.868
"Doctor Who"
is really big, you know?

00:04:06.868 --> 00:04:10.701
And we had a female Doctor Who,
and it was just like, "Yes!"

00:04:10.701 --> 00:04:12.367
That should
have happened years ago.

00:04:12.367 --> 00:04:15.100
But, anyway, I had such a
wonderful relationship

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:16.100
with Sean Connery.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:17.467
I did a
movie called "Entrapment"

00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:19.000
with him many, many years ago.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:22.534
And he was like
my elder brother, my dad,

00:04:22.534 --> 00:04:24.767
you know, my support.

00:04:24.767 --> 00:04:26.901
I just really loved him,
and we talked.

00:04:26.901 --> 00:04:28.534
I'm a Bond fanatic.

00:04:28.534 --> 00:04:30.334
And, so, he said to me once,

00:04:30.334 --> 00:04:32.434
"Yeah, you would have made
a great Bond Girl."

00:04:32.434 --> 00:04:35.167
I went, "A Bond Girl or Bond?"

00:04:35.167 --> 00:04:39.067
He went, "Bond...Girl."

00:04:39.067 --> 00:04:40.667
I went, "Oh, my gosh.

00:04:40.667 --> 00:04:42.067
I would have loved to
have played Bond."

00:04:42.067 --> 00:04:45.767
But I think those --
that time has passed.

00:04:45.767 --> 00:04:47.901
-Well, look, we are
lucky enough to see you

00:04:47.901 --> 00:04:50.801
in everything as it comes out,
and we're so happy anytime

00:04:50.801 --> 00:04:52.434
you show up, and it's just
wonderful to have you here.

00:04:52.434 --> 00:04:54.834
Thank you so much, as always.

00:04:54.834 --> 00:04:57.334
You guys, Catherine Zeta-Jones.

00:04:57.334 --> 00:04:58.634
The first two episodes of

00:04:58.634 --> 00:05:00.701
"National Treasure:
Edge of History"

00:05:00.701 --> 00:05:03.467
premieres December 14th
on Disney+,

00:05:03.467 --> 00:05:04.968
with new episodes
premiering Wednesdays.

00:05:04.968 --> 00:05:06.467
We'll be right back with
Hong Chau.

